{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"explore\\\",\n \\\"question\\\":\\\"What does the execution environment permit for socket operations? Specifically, can a harness bind a loopback port and an all-interfaces port, and what error does the runtime report when it cannot?\\\",\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Two recorded caveats depend on this environment fact and were previously inferred rather than observed: the startup-probe finding (a replica cannot be constructed where the fixed probe address cannot be dialled) and the failed attempt to measure the replica loop's startup phase (the listen attempt fails, so the loop dies on a nil listener under a non-verbose logger). The harness calls net.Listen once on 127.0.0.1:0 and once on 0.0.0.0:39451 and prints both outcomes. The local construction run reports 'socket: operation not permitted' for both, so the environment denies socket creation outright - which is consistent with the probe failure in the startup finding and with the loop attempt, and it means any check that needs a listener or a dial must treat that as an environment class rather than as target behaviour.\\\",\n \\\"harness_path\\\":\\\"explore_listen_test.go\\\",\n \\\"files\\\":{}}\njson.dump(sub,open('submission-explore-listen.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission submission-explore-listen.json 2>&1 | head -6\"","aggregated_output